---
title: "External Filter"
---

External filtering allows you to mix your own filtering logic with the grid's inbuilt filtering, without creating column-based filter components.

{% warning %}
This form of filtering is only compatible with the Client-Side Row Model, see [Row Models](./row-models/) for more details.
{% /warning %}

## Implementing External Filtering

The example below shows external filters in action. There are two methods on `gridOptions` you need to implement: `isExternalFilterPresent` and `doesExternalFilterPass`.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-options/properties.json" section="filter" names=["isExternalFilterPresent", "doesExternalFilterPass"] /%}

### isExternalFilterPresent

`isExternalFilterPresent` is called exactly once every time the grid senses a filter change. It should return `true` if external filtering is active or `false` otherwise. If you return `true`, `doesExternalFilterPass` will be called while filtering, otherwise `doesExternalFilterPass` will not be called.

### doesExternalFilterPass

`doesExternalFilterPass` is called once for each row node in the grid. If you return `false`, the node will be excluded from the final set.

## Re-running the External Filter

Both callbacks are reactive grid properties. The grid re-runs external filtering when you ask it to, and also when you hand it a new callback reference.

**Keep the callbacks stable and call `api.onFilterChanged()`.** Your filter state lives outside the grid, so change it and then tell the grid to filter again.

```js
ageType = 'below25';
api.onFilterChanged();
```

**Or supply a new callback reference.** `isExternalFilterPresent` and `doesExternalFilterPass` are reactive, so replacing either one with a new function re-runs filtering without an API call. Changing one is enough; you do not need to change both. See [Updating Grid Options](./grid-interface/#updating-grid-options) for how reactive properties are applied.

{% if isFramework("javascript") %}

```js
api.setGridOption('doesExternalFilterPass', (node) => node.data.age > 50);
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("angular") %}

```ts
// reassigning the field hands the grid a new reference, and filtering re-runs
this.doesExternalFilterPass = (node: IRowNode<IOlympicData>) => node.data!.age > this.minAge;
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("react") %}

```jsx
// a new reference on every change to minAge, so filtering re-runs
const doesExternalFilterPass = useCallback((node) => node.data.age > minAge, [minAge]);
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("vue") %}

```js
// reassigning the ref hands the grid a new reference, and filtering re-runs
doesExternalFilterPass.value = (node) => node.data.age > minAge.value;
```

{% /if %}

`doesExternalFilterPass` is only called while `isExternalFilterPresent` returns `true`, so the presence check must already reflect the new state — replacing the pass callback on its own filters nothing while the presence check still returns `false`.

Pick one of the two. A new callback reference _and_ a call to `api.onFilterChanged()` in the same update runs the filter twice.

{% if isFramework("javascript", "angular", "vue") %}

The example below takes the first route: the callbacks are stable, and each change to the filter state calls `api.onFilterChanged()`.

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("react") %}

The example below takes the second route: `ageType` is component state, and both callbacks list it as a dependency.

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("angular") %}

### Unstable Callback References

An Angular template binding is re-evaluated on every change-detection run. `.bind(this)` returns a new function each time it is evaluated, and a method call does the same whenever the method itself returns a function — so the grid re-filters on every cycle:

```html
<!-- each of these re-filters on every change-detection run -->
<ag-grid-angular [isExternalFilterPresent]="isExternalFilterPresent.bind(this)" />
<ag-grid-angular [isExternalFilterPresent]="getIsExternalFilterPresent()" />
```

Use an arrow class field instead and bind the field itself. The reference then stays the same for the lifetime of the component:

```ts
@Component({
    template: `<ag-grid-angular [isExternalFilterPresent]="isExternalFilterPresent" />`,
})
export class AppComponent {
    public ageType = 'everyone';

    public isExternalFilterPresent = (): boolean => this.ageType !== 'everyone';
}
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("react") %}

### Unstable Callback References

An inline lambda is a new function on every render, so the grid re-filters every time the component renders:

```jsx
// re-filters on every render
<AgGridReact doesExternalFilterPass={(node) => node.data.age > minAge} />
```

`useCallback` only helps if its dependency array covers the state the callback reads. A callback that reads a value missing from the array keeps the stale value, and one that lists a value it does not read churns its reference for no reason.

Where the filter state is held in `useState`, list that state on every callback that reads it. Each change then hands the grid new references and filtering re-runs, with no `api.onFilterChanged()` call:

```jsx
const [ageType, setAgeType] = useState('everyone');

const isExternalFilterPresent = useCallback(() => ageType !== 'everyone', [ageType]);
const doesExternalFilterPass = useCallback((node) => ageType === 'everyone' || node.data.age > 50, [ageType]);
```

Where it is held in a `useRef`, reads are always current, so the correct dependency array is empty and the reference never changes. Call `api.onFilterChanged()` after updating the ref:

```jsx
const ageType = useRef('everyone');

const isExternalFilterPresent = useCallback(() => ageType.current !== 'everyone', []);
const doesExternalFilterPass = useCallback((node) => ageType.current === 'everyone' || node.data.age > 50, []);
```

{% /if %}

